.TH CHGNOD 1M
.SH NAME
chgnod \- change current \s-1UNIX\s0 system nodename
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B chgnod 
[ new-name ] 
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Chgnod\^
accesses the structure
defined in \f3<sys/utsname.h>\fP:
.PP
.RS
.nf
struct utsname {
	char	sysname[9];
	char	nodename[9];
	char	release[9];
	char	version[9];
	char	machine[9];
};
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I Chgnod\^
with no arguments reports the settings of all of the above fields.
When supplied with one argument
.I chgnod\^
changes the nodename in the current running system.
.I Nodename\^
is a null terminated string containing the name that the system
is known by on a communications network.
.PP
New-name must be no longer than eight characters.
.SH EXAMPLE
.IP
chgnod funky
.PP
changes the nodename of your system to 'funky'.
.SH SEE ALSO
uname(2).
.SH NB
As this change has to be performed every time that the machine is rebooted,
it may be a good idea to put this command into /etc/rc.
